The head is a convolutional decoder that maps DINOv2 patch features to a $64{\times}64$ BEV occupancy grid. A linear projection maps each patch token to 256 channels, and the resulting sequence is reshaped into a $16{\times}16$ spatial map that preserves the patch grid layout. Two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) with batch normalization and GELU activations upsample this map to $64{\times}64$, and a final $1{\times}1$ convolution produces the binary occupancy logits. The convolutional architecture is a deliberate choice: because ViT patch tokens correspond to a regular spatial grid, transposed convolutions exploit adjacency structure that a position-agnostic MLP would ignore. This inductive bias allows the decoder to reconstruct fine-grained spatial boundaries from coarse patch features with fewer parameters.

We train this head with Focal Loss to handle the severe class imbalance between occupied and free cells:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{occ}} = -\alpha\,(1 - p_t)^{\gamma}\,\log(p_t),
\end{equation}
