\section{Discussion and Future Directions}
\label{sec:discussion}

The occupancy auxiliary head improves collision avoidance by reshaping the vision encoder's internal representations at zero inference cost. This implicit approach is effective across the SafeLIBERO benchmark, yet it does not provide formal safety guarantees. In dense clutter scenarios where multiple objects are tightly packed, implicit spatial awareness may be insufficient to prevent all collisions, because the policy must rely on the encoder having learned sufficiently fine-grained spatial boundaries for every possible configuration.

A natural extension is to construct an explicit safety layer that conditions on the spatial features already learned by the occupancy-trained encoder. We term this direction \textbf{OCC-CBF} (Occupancy-Conditioned Control Barrier Function): a learned barrier function $h(z_{\text{occ}}, x_{\text{robot}}) \geq 0$ that filters the VLA's proposed actions to maintain a safety invariant, where $z_{\text{occ}}$ denotes the encoder's spatial features and $x_{\text{robot}}$ the robot state. Unlike existing barrier function approaches that rely on hand-crafted geometric representations (Euclidean distances in AEGIS~\cite{hu2025aegis}, discrete occupancy grids in OGM-CBF~\cite{ji2024ogmcbf}, or object-centric decompositions in DCBF~\cite{kwon2026learningnudge}), OCC-CBF would operate directly in the VLA's continuous learned feature space. This avoids the need for explicit object segmentation or distance computation at test time and allows the barrier to reason about spatial relationships that the encoder has already learned to represent. We leave the formulation, training, and empirical validation of OCC-CBF to future work.

